{"reply":"In Chapter 5, Alice’s changing body makes identity feel unstable: when the Caterpillar asks, “Who are _you?_” she answers that she is no longer sure because she has “been changed several times” and is “many different sizes in a day.” Her physical changes disrupt her sense of continuity—she cannot simply point to a stable body and say, “That is me.”

The uncertainty also works in the other direction. Alice says, “I can’t remember things as I used,” and even familiar words come out differently. So her identity is unstable not only because her size changes, but because memory, language, and self-description no longer reliably confirm who she is. The two problems reinforce each other: bodily change makes the self feel unfamiliar, while the unfamiliar self makes each new bodily change more confusing.

